            "say": "like the th in think",
            "word": "θάλασσα",
            "word_latin": "thálasa",
            "meaning": "sea"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Ξ ξ",
            "latin": "x",
            "say": "like the x in taxi (ks)",
            "word": "ξένος",
            "word_latin": "xénos",
            "meaning": "foreigner"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Φ φ",
            "latin": "f",
            "say": "like the f in fun",
            "word": "φίλος",
            "word_latin": "fílos",
            "meaning": "friend"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Ψ ψ",
            "latin": "ps",
            "say": "like the ps in lapse, also at the start of a word",
            "word": "ψωμί",
            "word_latin": "psomí",
            "meaning": "bread"
          }
        ],
        "lang": "el"
      },
      {
        "h": "Two kinds of th"
      },
      "English writes *th* for two different sounds, and Greek has a letter for each: θ is the *th* of *think*, δ the *th* of *this*. In the readings of these lessons we write them *th* and *dh*.",
      {
        "h": "The soft γ"
      },
      "Before the sounds *e* and *i* (ε, η, ι, υ) the γ is read like the *y* in *yes*: *γέλιο* (yélio, laughter), *γη* (yi, earth). Before *a*, *o*, *u* and before consonants it is a soft *g*, made at the back of the throat without closing it: *γάτα* (gháta, cat), *γράμμα* (ghráma, letter).",
      {
        "h": "Double letters"
      },
      "A double consonant is read as one: *θάλασσα* is *thálasa*, *γράμμα* is *ghráma*.",
      {
        "h": "The whole alphabet"
